From: quickjs-ng bundle work
Subject: Register bundles: compact wire format, fused decoder, no stack bytecode

What this does
  Splits the register bytecode into two encodings (bundle plan D1/D2). The exec
  encoding is what the VM runs and does not change. The wire encoding is what
  goes in a bundle: compact, variable length. JS_WriteFunctionTag converts
  exec -> wire with regvm_wire_encode; JS_ReadFunctionBody converts wire -> exec
  with regvm_wire_decode in one pass, interning atoms, validating every operand,
  computing args_captured/argv_safe, building the instruction-start bitmap and
  checking branch targets. That single pass replaces regvm_map_atoms2 x2 plus
  regvm_validate_code on the read path.

  The register program moves out of the function header into the lazy region
  (D4), and the stack bytecode stops being written at all: a register bundle has
  byte_code_len = 0, ic_count = 0 and stack_size = 0. The lazy region is now
  vardefs, cpool, wire register code, objlit templates (no stack pc) and debug
  (register-keyed pc2line). REGVM_FORMAT_VERSION goes 15 -> 16.

  Wire operands (D3): opcode 1 byte; a register operand is a ULEB128 register
  index for the wide form and the raw stored byte (a byte offset) for the narrow
  form; an atom is the tagged-int/bundle-index ULEB that bc_put_atom/bc_get_atom
  already use; a branch offset is the engine's zigzag "sleb128" of the same
  relative offset; u/s and I/c are ULEB128/zigzag-LEB.

Why we need it
  A register bundle used to embed both the stack bytecode and a copy of the exec
  register program, so it was 1.45-1.62x the stack bundle and the reader walked
  the register program several times (map atoms twice, validate twice). Moving
  the code to the lazy region in a compact form and folding the reader's work
  into one decode removes the stack bytecode from memory and the bundle, and
  makes the read a single linear pass.

What it adds
  quickjs.c
      regvm_wire_put_atom, regvm_wire_encode, regvm_wire_get_leb,
      regvm_wire_get_sleb, regvm_wire_decode; the BCReaderState wire_is_start/
      wire_targets/wire_scratch_len scratch (reused across functions); the
      JS_WriteFunctionTag and JS_ReadFunctionBody/JS_ReadFunctionTag layout
      changes; the objlit-without-pc reader/writer paths; the in-decode
      regvm_all_threaded computation (D13).
  quickjs-regvm.h
      REGVM_FORMAT_VERSION 15 -> 16.

  Measured on x86-64, clang-19 -O2, taskset -c 5, .local/coldoutline/octane-fixed
  (stack sizes in parentheses):
      pdfjs    4,264,731 bytes vs 3,872,207 stack = 1.101x
      mandreel 9,167,743 bytes vs 8,476,587 stack = 1.081x
      box2d      701,452 bytes vs   706,086 stack = 0.994x
      pdfjs eager load, 30 loads, ldread: 0.37 s (was 0.77 s before this patch;
      stack 0.20 s). pdfjs lazy load, 30 loads: 0.08 s (target 0.08 s).
      The wire decode is ~0.25 s of the eager load; a no-decode build reads the
      same bundle in 0.12 s.
  Wire bytes per operand kind for pdfjs (from a temporary instrumentation of
  regvm_wire_encode, not committed): total 1,228,306 = op 291,346, r 487,396,
  I 193,163, a 162,238, u 29,842, b 26,491, i 13,022, c 11,465, t 6,779,
  s 6,564.

  Gates: box2d 546.1008391043414 from source, eager bundle and lazy bundle;
  test262 50/81192 with no leaks; differential corpus 400/0/0; all 13 Octane
  rows run in all three modes; ASan of run.c clean on box2d and six Octane rows
  (eager and lazy). box2d run-time instructions 3.336 G vs 3.361 G before the
  format change (G3 -0.76%); box2d eager vs source run-only instructions within
  0.7% (D13 gate).

  Deviations recorded in the plan's Findings: the pdfjs bundle is 1.101x rather
  than the 1.10x target (the register pc2line and the wire are each a little
  larger than the plan's estimate), and the pdfjs eager load is 0.37 s rather
  than the 0.30 s target.

